WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.753
The payments topic has six partitions, six separate logs.

2
00:00:06.753 --> 00:00:09.810
At peak they carry 3,000 payments a second.

3
00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.725
One ledger consumer can book about 1,000.

4
00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:16.069
So you run several consumers in one consumer group.

5
00:00:16.069 --> 00:00:21.971
The group splits the partitions between its members: each partition goes to exactly one of them.

6
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:27.533
With two members, each one reads three partitions, 1,500 payments a second.

7
00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:31.088
That's more than it can book, so both fall behind.

8
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.005
You start a third consumer.

9
00:00:33.005 --> 00:00:41.276
It joins the group, and the group coordinator, a broker that tracks the members, starts a rebalance: a new split of the partitions.

10
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.699
First it revokes two partitions.

11
00:00:43.699 --> 00:00:48.442
Consumer 1 gives up partition 2, and consumer 2 gives up partition 3.

12
00:00:48.442 --> 00:00:53.872
Each one commits its offset, its bookmark in that partition, before letting go.

13
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:59.648
Then the coordinator assigns both to consumer 3, which starts at the committed offsets.

14
00:00:59.648 --> 00:01:02.199
The other four partitions never stopped.

15
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.871
Now every member reads two partitions, about 1,000 payments a second, and the backlog drains.

16
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.801
Then consumer 3 crashes.

17
00:01:10.801 --> 00:01:15.620
A live consumer sends the coordinator a heartbeat every few seconds.

18
00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:17.675
Consumer 3's heartbeats stop.

19
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:26.419
After the session timeout, 45 seconds by default, the coordinator removes it and moves partitions 2 and 3 to the survivors.

20
00:01:26.633 --> 00:01:29.313
They start from the last committed offsets.

21
00:01:29.313 --> 00:01:35.671
Any payment consumer 3 read after its last commit gets read again, so the ledger must spot duplicates.

22
00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:37.674
Why one reader per partition?

23
00:01:37.674 --> 00:01:41.954
If two members read the same log, both would book every payment in it.

24
00:01:41.954 --> 00:01:44.645
One owner reads each payment once, in order.

25
00:01:44.867 --> 00:01:46.534
That also caps the group.

26
00:01:46.534 --> 00:01:49.534
Six partitions keep at most six members busy.

27
00:01:49.534 --> 00:01:51.201
A seventh would sit idle.
